from ss import *
L(6,'social-science','india-that-is-bharat','India, That Is Bharat',
 ('What is in a name?','Your name tells people who you are. A country also has names, and India has had several. Some were given by Indians themselves and some by travellers from other lands. The names show how people saw this land over thousands of years. In this chapter we trace them.'),
 [('The land and its past',['Today India is a modern nation with defined borders, states and a known population. It looked very different 500, 2,000 or 5,000 years ago.','The wider region is called the Indian Subcontinent. Its natural boundaries are the Himalayan mountains in the north and the seas in the south, east and west, and it has had many names and shifting boundaries.']),
  ('How Indians named India',['The Rig Veda, India\'s most ancient text, calls the northwest region Sapta Sindhava, the land of the seven rivers. The word Sindhava comes from Sindhu, the Indus river.','Later texts name other regions. The Mahabharata uses Bharatavarsha and Jambudvipa for the whole Subcontinent, and scholars place the poem from a few centuries BCE onward.','Bharatavarsha means the country of the Bharatas. Bharata is a name first found in the Rig Veda for one of the main groups of people. Jambudvipa means the island of the jamun fruit tree, a tree native to India.','A few centuries later Bharata became the usual name. The Vishnu Purana describes it as the country north of the ocean and south of the snowy mountains. Different parts of the country had a similar idea of India, which shows that ancient Indians knew their geography well.','The name Bharat remains in use today and is also written Bharatam in south India. The Constitution of India, which came into force in 1950, mentions both India and Bharat.']),
  ('How foreigners named India',['The first foreigners to mention India were the Persians. In the 6th century BCE a Persian emperor took control of the Indus region. Because their language had no initial h sound, Sindhu became Hindu, and the Greeks changed it further to Indoi or Indike. From this came the word India.','The ancient Chinese called India Yintu or Yindu and also Tianzhu, which could be read as heavenly master, showing respect for the land of the Buddha.','Hindustan was used in a Persian inscription some 1,800 years ago, and later most invaders used the word for the Subcontinent.']),
  ('Why people travelled to India',['Travellers came to India for trade, for knowledge and for religion. The Chinese monk Xuanzang visited many parts of India, met scholars, collected Buddhist texts and returned to China after 17 years, where he translated them from Sanskrit into Chinese.','Many other scholars came over the centuries. Their records are valuable sources of history that tell us what India looked like to outsiders.'])],
 [('Match the names with the people who used them: Sapta Sindhava, Hindu, Yindu, Bharatavarsha.',[('Sapta Sindhava is a Vedic name, used by early Indians.','It comes from the Rig Veda.'),('Hindu comes from Sindhu and was used by the Persians.','They dropped the h sound for Sindhu.'),('Yindu was used by the Chinese and Bharatavarsha by the Mahabharata.','Use the source of each name.')])],
 ('Why does the Subcontinent have so many names?',['Different peoples named it in their own languages.','Names changed with time and with who was describing it.','Many names come from Sindhu, the Indus.'],['Thinking India and Bharat are two unrelated countries.','Thinking Hindu is a modern name only.']),
 ['India has been called Sapta Sindhava, Bharatavarsha, Jambudvipa, Bharata, Hindu, Indike, Yindu and Hindustan.','The name India comes from Sindhu through the Persians and Greeks.','Chinese travellers like Xuanzang came to study in India.','Bharat is both an ancient name and the name used in the Constitution.'],
 [('m','Which text names the region Sapta Sindhava?','The Rig Veda','The Constitution','A Persian inscription','Sapta Sindhava means the land of the seven rivers.'),
  ('m','Bharatavarsha means','the country of the Bharatas','island of the jamun tree','land of the seven rivers','It comes from the name Bharata.'),
  ('m','The word India comes from','Sindhu','Bharata','Jambu','Persians and Greeks changed Sindhu to Hindu and Indike.'),
  ('t','Foreign travellers had no part in naming India.',False,'Persians, Greeks and Chinese gave their own names.'),
  ('m','Jambudvipa means','the island of the jamun fruit tree','land of seven rivers','heavenly master','Jambu is the jamun tree.'),
  ('m','The Chinese monk who stayed 17 years in India was','Xuanzang','Aryabhata','Alexander','He collected Buddhist texts and took them to China.'),
  ('m','Tianzhu was a Chinese word for India that could mean','heavenly master','seven rivers','island of fruit','It showed respect for the land of the Buddha.'),
  ('t','The Constitution of India uses both India and Bharat.',True,'Both names are in use today.'),
  ('m','The Persians called the Indus region Hindu because they','did not have the initial h sound','liked a new name','were ruled by Indians','Sindhu became Hindu in their speech.'),
  ('m','Which phrase describes Bharata in the Vishnu Purana?','North of the ocean and south of the snowy mountains','Land of seven rivers','Island of fruit','This description matches the Subcontinent.')],
 keep=['Bharat','Sindhu','Subcontinent'])
